<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Feb 20, 2023">
<CPscript title="The Discrete Schwarz Lemma" level="min" screenmode="map" >
<description>This script pursues the monotonicities observed in "MaxPackHyp.xmd", leading to the discrete Schwarz Lemma.</description>
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" iconname="bookmark.png">act 0;Cleanse;infile_read blob.p;copy 1;max_pack;Disp -w -c -cfc50 A;Disp -p1 -w -c -cfc50 A;Map 0 1; </cmd>
<text> Our concern is with discrete analytic self maps of the unit disc. Thus f: Q --> P where Q is a maximal packing of the unit disc and the image P also lies in the unit disc. It is convenient to start with P and then build Q for the domain by computing the maximal packing for the complex of P.

In the maximal packing, the boundary circles are horocycles (technically, infinite radius, but in practice, hyperbolic radius 5.0 is enough). Just repacking is too easy. Instead, let's make the boundary circles incrementally larger and repack as we go. Simply press the 'u' key. </text>
<cmd name="u" mnemonic="u" tooltip="increment the boundary and repack" iconname="list.png">[1];[2];disp -p1 -wr </cmd>
<text> For an analytic self map F of the disc with F(0) = 0, the classical Schwarz Lemma tells us that |F'(0)| is less than or equal to 1 with equality if and only if F is a rotation. 

Note that our discrete analytic function f satisfies parallel hypotheses, including f(0) = 0, since the blue 'alpha' vertex is at the origin in both domain p0 and range p1. The discrete Schwarz Lemma says that f^# (the analogue of |F'|) is less than or equal to 1, with equality if and only if f is a rotation (i.e., if and only ifp1 is just a rotated copy of p0).

This script is intended to suggest why this is true. So start with the first command of the script again and watch the blue circle in p1 as you press 'u'. What does the blue circle do? Why?

You might note that 'u' is actually two commands: '1' and '2'. What '1' does is increase all boundary radii by 10 percent. Then '2' does 'rld' (repack/layout/display). Running through the process with these steps --- it may help to zoom in on 'alpha' in p1. Try to mesh the computations with the monotonicity results illustrated in "MaxHypPack.xmd" and you may see where this larger monotonicity comes from.

I hope you have noted, then, that the blue circle is always increasing until you reach the maximal packing. In particular, it must have started out smaller in p1. This explains the discrete Schwarz Lemma, Theorem 6.8 of the book. </text>
<Section title="Tools">
<cmd inline="no" name="1" mnemonic="1" tooltip="increment the boundary circles" iconname="format_increaseindent.png">adjust_rad -p1 1.1 b;disp -p1 -c b; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="2" mnemonic="2" tooltip="repack and display" iconname="editdelete.png">rld -p1;disp -p1 -wr; </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
